--TEST--
Core\IO::temporaryDir answers a directory that already exists and is already empty, and two calls never answer the same one — so a program never races another for a name, which is the whole of what `sys_get_temp_dir` plus a hand-rolled suffix gets wrong — spec § 14
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Nothing here prints a path: the answer is host-specific by construction, so
// what is asserted is every property of it that is not. The first is the one
// PHP's split cannot offer -- the directory is *made*, so it is there before
// the caller sees the name, and there is no window in which somebody else can
// take it.
string $first = Core\IO::temporaryDir();

if (Core\IO::exists($first)) {
    echo "made: yes\n";
} else {
    echo "made: no\n";
}

// Empty, and asserted the only way emptiness can be asserted with the members
// that exist: `removeDir` refuses a directory holding anything, so one that
// removes on the first call held nothing.
string $second = Core\IO::temporaryDir();

if ($first == $second) {
    echo "distinct: no\n";
} else {
    echo "distinct: yes\n";
}

Core\IO::removeDir($second);

if (Core\IO::exists($second)) {
    echo "second removed: no\n";
} else {
    echo "second removed: yes\n";
}

// And it is a place to put things, which is the reason it is a directory
// rather than a file: the round trip below is the whole point of the member.
string $note = Core\Path::join($first, "note.txt");
Core\IO::write($note, "hello\n");
int $size = Core\IO::size($note) as int;
echo "wrote: ", $size as string, "\n";

// The program removes what it made -- nothing sweeps this for it, because a
// runtime deciding that lifetime would be deciding it for data it knows
// nothing about.
Core\IO::remove($note);
Core\IO::removeDir($first);

if (Core\IO::exists($first)) {
    echo "first removed: no\n";
} else {
    echo "first removed: yes\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = true
write = true
--EXPECT--
made: yes
distinct: yes
second removed: yes
wrote: 6
first removed: yes
